.TH VALET-KEY 1 "2026-09-09" "valet-key 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
valet-key \- profile-aware, concurrent-safe launcher for coding agents
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B claude
.RI [ agent-args ...]
.br
.B valet-key run
.I agent
.RI [ agent-args ...]
.br
.B valet-key
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B valet-key
wraps a coding agent (Claude Code, and others via adapters) so that every
launch is placed in the right
.B profile
(a logical identity such as
.IR work ,
.IR personal ,
.IR client-a ),
guarded for coherence, and given an isolated credential slot before the real
agent is exec'd.
.PP
You do not normally type
.BR valet-key .
You install per-agent
.B shims
\(em symlinks named
.BR claude ,
.BR codex ,
.BR gemini ,
.RB \&... \" ...
that point at the engine \(em and put the shims directory first on
.BR PATH .
The engine reads its own invoked name
.RB ( $0 )
to pick the agent (the busybox / git multi-call pattern). Invoked as
.B valet-key
it is the management CLI.
.PP
Each launch runs four steps:
.IP 1. 3
.B resolve
the active profile from the current directory / context;
.IP 2. 3
.B guard
coherence (proceed, warn, or refuse) before launching;
.IP 3. 3
.B lease
a private credential slot from the profile's pool (pooled agents only; a
poolless agent uses the profile's directory directly), so concurrent sessions
never share one rotating refresh token;
.IP 4. 3
.B exec
the real agent with the slot as its config directory.
.PP
The concurrency problem it solves: some agents keep a single credentials file
whose OAuth refresh token is single-use and rotates on every refresh. Two
sessions sharing it invalidate each other, forcing periodic re-logins. A pool
of thin slot directories \(em each sharing the account's state but owning a
private, independently-logged-in credentials file \(em removes the race. An
agent whose refresh token is stable, or which uses no rotating credential, is
.B poolless
\(em valet-key gives it profile isolation without a pool.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Running
.TP
.B claude " [" \fIargs\fR ...]
Run Claude in the resolved profile on a leased slot (the shim form). Any
installed shim behaves the same for its agent.
.TP
.B valet-key run \fIagent\fR " [" \fIargs\fR ...]
The same, naming the agent explicitly.
.SS Pools
.TP
.B valet-key provision \fIagent\fR " [" \fIprofile\fR "] [" \fIN\fR ]
Create or refresh a slot pool of
.I N
slots (default 5) for
.IR agent / profile .
Slots ship cold \(em the login is lazy; the first session to reach a cold slot
logs in once.
.IP
It CONVERGES on
.IR N ,
creating what is missing and removing what is surplus, so re-running it is a
resize rather than a high-water mark. A surplus slot that is LEASED or WARM is
kept rather than discarded \(em a live session is using the one, and the other
holds a login someone sat through a browser flow for \(em and what could not
be removed is reported.
.I N
is recorded in the pool, which is what lets
.B check
audit the size at all.
.TP
.B valet-key login \fR[\fIaction\fR] [\fIagent\fR] [\fIprofile\fR]
Manage logins for the resolved (or named) pool.
.I agent
defaults to
.BR claude .
For
.IR profile ,
see
.B WHICH PROFILE
below. The
.I action
is one of
.BR warm ", " check ", " force ", " stale .
.B warm
(default) logs into the next cold slot;
.B check
lists warm/total and each slot's rotation age and days-to-cap;
.B force
re-logs the oldest warm slot;
.B stale
re-logs every slot within
.B VALET_KEY_SLOT_STALE_DAYS
of its cap.
.TP
.B valet-key stale
Across all pools, list every warm slot within
.B VALET_KEY_SLOT_STALE_DAYS
of its hard OAuth cap, one
.RI < id >/< slot >
.IR days d
per line. Read-only; launches nothing. Its line count is a natural signal for a
login-reminder banner.
.TP
.B valet-key check \fR[\fIagent\fR " [" \fIprofile\fR "]]"
Audit one pool, or with no argument the whole provisioned setup (every pool).
Reports slot / warm / leased counts and exits non-zero on structural drift: a
missing pool, a dangling shared link, or a slot count that no longer matches
the
.I N
.B provision
was last asked for. This is the declarative drift audit; see
.B doctor
for the environmental one.
.TP
.B valet-key reconcile \fR[\fIagent\fR "[" \fIprofile\fR "]] [" \-\-check ]
Converge the agent's SHARED-BUT-AGENT-WRITTEN files across the pool: the base
config directory and every slot.
.B \-\-check
reports which copies are behind and exits non-zero, changing nothing.
.IP
Some state an agent keeps is not per-session at all. Claude Code records
per-project trust, allowed tools and MCP approvals in
.IR .claude.json ,
which is a PRIVATE COPY per slot \(em it has to be, because the agent
rewrites it constantly and sessions sharing one file would interleave
read-modify-write. The consequence is that the same repository asks to be
trusted again on every slot it has not been opened in, and a newly provisioned
slot starts knowing nothing.
.IP
So the copies stay private and are RECONCILED instead. That is strictly safer
than sharing one file: every writer still owns its own, and the worst case is
a merge that loses a cycle and is redone on the next one rather than a
corrupted config.
.IP
The merge is LAST-WRITER-WINS per project, NOT a union. Trust and allowed
tools live inside a project entry, so unioning them would mean that revoking
trust, or removing a tool, gets silently restored from a stale copy on another
slot \(em a mechanism that resurrects revoked permissions is worse than the
problem it solves. Entries are taken whole from the newest copy; the union is
only ACROSS projects, so a new one propagates and an edit (including a
removal) wins.
.IP
Identity never moves, even between slots of one pool, and never between pools:
the account is not shared state. Nor are counters and caches, for which a
merge would be meaningless.
.IP
A launch already reconciles the slot it is about to use, so this verb is the
outward half \(em valet-key
.BR exec s
the agent and is therefore never around when a session ends to push what it
learned to the other slots. Run it from a provisioning pass, a timer, or by
hand.
.TP
.B valet-key resolve \fR[\fIagent\fR]
DRY RUN: report what a launch HERE would do, and launch nothing. Prints the
resolved profile, the config directory, and the pool behind it; then shows the
selection chain (which hook answered, and which were never asked because an
earlier one did) and each guard's verdict. Exits
.B 1
if a guard would refuse, so
.RB ' "valet-key resolve && ..." '
means what it looks like.
.IP
Without it the only way to learn which account a directory routes to was to
launch the agent and read the announce line off its standard error \(em to run
the thing you were trying to check first \(em and a surprising answer had
nothing to inspect.
.TP
.B valet-key doctor
Environment health sweep, read-only. Checks that each agent command actually
routes through valet-key (the shim wins on
.BR PATH ),
that no agent API key is leaking into the environment (it would override a
slot's login), that no pool is saturated (every slot leased, so the next launch
overflows to the shared base), that no warm slot is near its OAuth cap, that
no profile you can actually reach is running WITHOUT a pool (with none, every
session for it shares one credentials file, exactly as if valet-key were not
installed \(em and
.B check
cannot see this, because it audits pools that exist), and that the installed
no pool's shared state has fallen out of step across its slots (see
.BR reconcile ),
and that the installed
hooks are actually being read and are answering inside their contract (see
.BR "THE HOOK SEAMS" ).
Exits non-zero only on a BREAKAGE \(em a shim that does not intercept, or a
hook that is not running or is out of contract; the rest are advisories, so a
red line always means something is broken. Complements
.B check
(declarative drift) as a readiness probe complements a provisioner.
.TP
.B valet-key slots \fIverb\fR " [" \fIargs\fR ...]
Escape hatch to the raw slot-pool library (see
.BR FILES ).
The library's own verbs are
.BR provision ", " lease ", " warm ", " login-check ", " force ", "
.BR login-stale ", " sync ", and " check ,
each taking the pool id
.RI < agent >/< profile >
and that profile's base config directory;
.B counts
takes the pool id alone;
.B stale
takes an optional agent to scan (its cap policy is per-agent, so an unfiltered
scan can only be right for one agent at a time); and
.B pools
takes no arguments.
.IP
This verb does NOT load the agent's adapter. Everything the library needs
beyond those arguments \(em which file to isolate, which to share, which
process name identifies a live holder \(em normally arrives from the adapter
through the environment, so a bare
.B slots
call silently falls back to the built-in defaults, which are Claude Code's. On
another agent that means the wrong credential file, and therefore wrong warm
counts. Use it for debugging, and prefer the wrapping commands.
.SS Shims
.TP
.B valet-key shim \fIagent\fR ...
Create a shim for each named agent in the shims directory \(em a symlink to the
engine, so invoking that name routes here. Warns if the shims directory is not
first on
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B valet-key unshim \fIagent\fR ...
Remove those shims.
.TP
.B valet-key shims
List the current shims.
.TP
.B valet-key init
Print the shell line that prepends the shims directory to
.BR PATH .
Add it to your profile:
.PP
.RS
.EX
eval "$(valet-key init)"
.EE
.RE
.TP
.B valet-key shims-dir
Print the shims directory.
.TP
.B valet-key rehash
Re-point every existing shim at the current engine (after moving valet-key).
.SS Help
.TP
.B valet-key help
Print the usage summary (also the output of
.B valet-key
with no arguments, or with
.BR \-h / \-\-help ).
.SH PROFILES
A
.B profile
is a logical identity, not an agent path. Each agent
.B adapter
grounds it in that agent's config directory: the default profile maps to the
adapter's base directory, any other
.I P
to
.RI < base >- P .
For Claude Code that is
.I ~/.claude
for
.I personal
and
.I ~/.claude-work
for
.IR work .
The default profile is
.I personal
.RB ( VALET_KEY_DEFAULT_PROFILE
overrides).
.PP
A profile may instead be pinned to an explicit directory by a
.RI < agent >
.RI < profile >
.RI < dir >
line in
.IR $VALET_KEY_CONFIG/dirs ,
overriding the
.RI < base >- P
default \(em for a config dir that must live at a specific path, or a tool
whose environment variable points at a home rather than a config directory.
.SH WHICH PROFILE
A profile named on the command line is used as given. OMITTED, it is RESOLVED
exactly as a launch from the same directory would resolve it \(em so
.B provision
.RI ( check ", " login ", " resolve )
run inside a work tree act on the WORK pool.
.PP
These used to disagree:
.B login
resolved from context while
.B provision
and
.B check
fell back to the default profile. So
.RB ' "cd ~/work && valet-key provision claude" '
built the PERSONAL pool while
.RB ' "valet-key login" '
one line later acted on WORK, and nothing said so \(em the pool you thought
you had made was somewhere else. A tool whose whole job is choosing an account
cannot hold two answers to where "here" is.
.PP
Because the answer now depends on context, each of those commands PRINTS the
pool it settled on. Resolution you cannot see is resolution you cannot check;
.B valet-key resolve
shows the reasoning behind it.
.PP
A profile name is validated as a DNS label wherever it enters \(em from a
hook, from the
.I profiles
table, or from the command line \(em because it becomes a directory component
and half a pool id on every one of those paths. An invalid one is an error,
never a silent fall back to the default.
.SH THE HOOK SEAMS
valet-key must decide two things it cannot always know: WHICH PROFILE applies
here, and WHETHER IT MAY LAUNCH at all. Its own answers are the directory table
in
.I profiles
and "always proceed". Both are extension points, and they are separate because
they are different questions: a veto is not a profile name, and a selection
cannot say "stop".
.PP
A hook's DIRECTORY says which question it answers, so there is no verb to
dispatch and nothing forces you to answer a question you do not care about. All
hooks run in name order with
.B VALET_KEY_AGENT
in the environment.
.TP
.I $VALET_KEY_HOOKS/profile.d/*
SELECTION. Each runs until one ANSWERS. Exit
.B 0
printing a name selects that profile; exit
.B 0
printing NOTHING is also an answer \(em "definitely the default here" \(em and
stops the chain; a NON-ZERO exit means "I cannot tell" and passes to the next
hook. Only that last case falls through, and after every hook abstains the
directory table runs.
.IP
That distinction is the point. A provider that knows the answer is the baseline
can say so instead of inventing a token for the default, and a BROKEN hook
cannot be mistaken for one that deliberately said "baseline". The name is
validated as a DNS label; an invalid one is a hard error, never a silent fall
back.
.TP
.I $VALET_KEY_HOOKS/guard.d/*
VETO, with the chosen profile as
.RI $ 1
and in
.BR VALET_KEY_PROFILE .
EVERY hook runs and ANY refusal refuses: exit
.B 0
to proceed,
.B 2
to warn and continue,
.B 1
to refuse and abort. Anything else is the hook failing, and counts as a
REFUSAL \(em a safety check that could not run has not cleared anything. The
hook owns the message (its standard error passes through).
.IP
Any-refusal-wins is not a style choice: adding a guard must only ever make
things stricter, or a second guard could silently cancel the first. That is
what lets two unrelated vetoes \(em a boundary and a VPN check \(em compose
without either knowing the other exists.
.PP
A hook is only consulted if it is EXECUTABLE; a file without the bit is
skipped in silence by both seams.
.B doctor
reports one as a failure for that reason \(em it looks installed, and it has
never run.
.PP
valet-key ships NO hooks and needs none: with none installed it runs a single
.I personal
profile, consults
.I profiles
if you wrote one, and refuses nothing. Annotated examples \(em routing by git
remote or kube context, refusing on a downed VPN, warning on battery \(em are
in
.IR <share>/hooks/ ,
to copy and edit. Nothing there is installed, and none of it is about any
particular integration.
.PP
The adapter says
.I how
a profile maps to config; the seams say
.I which
profile and
.I whether it is allowed.
A guard is a reminder, not a wall: it reflects and refuses but does not
enforce. A real boundary belongs in the OS \(em ownership, an ACL, a namespace
\(em in whatever supplies the hook. valet-key can say no; it cannot BE the
lock.
.SH ADAPTERS
An adapter is a shell fragment under
.I libexec/adapters/<agent>
sourced by the engine. It declares the config-dir environment variable
.RB ( ADAPTER_ENV ),
the base config directory
.RB ( ADAPTER_BASE ),
whether the agent needs slot isolation
.RB ( ADAPTER_SLOTS ),
the slot file policy
.RB ( ADAPTER_CRED_FILE ,
.BR ADAPTER_STATIC_FILES ,
.BR ADAPTER_SEED_FILES ),
the agent-written files that should nonetheless be common to the pool
.RB ( ADAPTER_MERGE_FILES ,
reconciled by
.IR libexec/merge/<agent> ),
the command-name fragment that identifies a live lease holder
.RB ( ADAPTER_PROC_MATCH ,
required on a pooled agent \(em without it any process at all looks like a
live holder, and a lease could be reclaimed from a running session after a
PID is reused),
the login and cap knobs
.RB ( ADAPTER_UNSET_ENV ,
env scrubbed so a stray API key cannot override the stored login;
.BR ADAPTER_LOGIN_ARGS ,
extra args to reach the login flow when warming;
.BR ADAPTER_EXP_PATTERN ,
the slot cap pattern, empty for "cap unknown";
.BR ADAPTER_QUIET ),
and functions
.B adapter_realbin
(find the real binary, skipping the shim) and
.B adapter_preexec
(launch-time environment). Add an agent by dropping in an adapter.
.PP
Bundled adapters:
.B claude
and
.B codex
are pooled (single-use OAuth token);
.B gemini
is poolless (stable token, profile isolation only); and
.B gcloud
is poolless too \(em a non-agent identity CLI, showing valet-key generalizes
beyond coding agents.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VALET_KEY_CONFIG
Config directory. Default
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/valet-key ,
else
.IR ~/.config/valet-key .
.TP
.B VALET_KEY_SHIMS_DIR
Where shims live. Default
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/valet-key/shims ,
else
.IR ~/.local/share/valet-key/shims .
.TP
.B VALET_KEY_HOOKS
The hooks root holding
.I profile.d/
and
.IR guard.d/ .
Default
.IR $VALET_KEY_CONFIG/hooks .
.TP
.B VALET_KEY_DEFAULT_PROFILE
The unmatched fallback profile. Default
.IR personal .
.TP
.B VALET_KEY_POOL_ROOT
Slot-pool root. Default
.IR ~/.valet-key-pool .
.TP
.B VALET_KEY_SLOT_STALE_DAYS
Cap-warning window for
.B stale
and
.BR "login stale" .
Default 7.
.TP
.B VALET_KEY_VERBOSE
When non-empty, print the launch line even for an adapter that declares itself
quiet
.RB ( ADAPTER_QUIET ,
e.g.
.BR gcloud ,
whose callers parse its standard error). Loud adapters print it regardless.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set, the
.BR check ", " doctor ", and " setup.sh
markers are emitted plain rather than coloured. They are also plain whenever
standard output is not a terminal, so a pipe needs no flag.
.TP
.BI < AGENT >_BIN
Override an adapter's resolved binary (odd install or testing):
.BR CLAUDE_BIN ", " CODEX_BIN ", " GEMINI_BIN ", " GCLOUD_BIN .
The path is VERIFIED to be an executable file, and a bad one is an error
rather than something handed on: an unchecked override surfaced much later and
in disguise, as a slot login reporting "still cold" with no hint that the
binary it tried to run was not there.
The pool size is the
.I N
argument to
.BR provision ,
not an environment variable.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B claude \fR/\fB valet-key run
The real agent's own status, since valet-key
.BR exec s
it. A guard refusal aborts before that with
.BR 1 .
.TP
.B valet-key check
.B 0
when every audited pool is present and structurally sound,
.B 1
on a missing pool, a dangling shared link, a slot count that no longer matches
the recorded size, or a pool whose agent has no adapter.
.TP
.B valet-key resolve
.B 0
if a launch here would proceed,
.B 1
if a guard would refuse it.
.TP
.B valet-key doctor
.B 0
unless a real breakage was found (an agent command that does not route through
valet-key, a retired hook file still in place, a hook that is not being read
or is out of contract).  Warnings \(em a leaked API key, a saturated pool, a
profile with no pool, a slot near its cap \(em are advisories and do not
change the status.
.TP
.B setup.sh check
.B 0
when the install links are all present and pointing at this tree,
.B 1
on drift.
.PP
Everything else exits
.B 0
on success and
.B 1
on a usage or lookup error (an unknown command, an agent with no adapter, a
profile name that is not a usable label); the raw
.B slots
library exits
.B 2
on an unknown verb.
.SH OUTPUT
.BR check ", " doctor ", and " setup.sh
speak a plain MARKER contract \(em lines beginning
.BR [OK] ", " [FAIL] ", " [WARN] ", or " [IGNORE]
\(em so an integrator can restyle them in its own palette while folding the
exit status into its own report. Standalone at a terminal valet-key colours
them itself; piped, or under
.BR NO_COLOR ,
they stay plain. The markers are a string convention, not a dependency on any
integrator's styling code.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/valet-key
The engine. Self-locates
.I libexec
as a sibling of its own resolved path, so an install is a symlink and needs no
configuration.
.I share
holds templates and examples for a human; nothing in the engine reads it.
.TP
.I libexec/slots
The credential-slot pool library.
.TP
.I libexec/adapters/<agent>
Per-agent adapters. One file here is one agent valet-key knows about, which is
what
.BR shim ", " check " and " doctor
enumerate.
.TP
.I libexec/merge/<agent>
The per-agent reconciler for that agent's
.BR ADAPTER_MERGE_FILES .
The engine owns WHICH directories and WHEN; the FORMAT is the helper's,
because valet-key is POSIX shell with no runtime dependencies and merging a
config document is not shell work. An agent with no helper simply has no
reconciliation.
.TP
.I libexec/adapter.sh
Helpers shared by every adapter, sourced before them \(em notably the real-
binary lookup, so the
.BI < AGENT >_BIN
check and the never-return-the-shim rule have one implementation rather than
one per agent. Deliberately NOT in
.IR adapters/ ,
where it would become a phantom agent.
.TP
.I setup.sh
The package's own installer: links
.IR bin ,
.IR libexec ,
.IR share ,
and the man pages into a prefix
.RB ( install ),
removes those links
.RB ( uninstall ),
audits them
.RB ( check ),
runs the in-repo suite
.RB ( test ),
or prints the packaged version
.RB ( version ).
.I PREFIX
(default
.IR ~/.local )
and the
.I XDG_*
variables choose the destination.
.TP
.I <share>/hooks/
Annotated example hooks to copy and edit. NOTHING there is installed.
.TP
.I <share>/profiles
.TQ
.I <share>/dirs
Commented templates for the two configuration tables below.
.TP
.I $VALET_KEY_CONFIG/hooks/profile.d/
.TQ
.I $VALET_KEY_CONFIG/hooks/guard.d/
The optional integrator hooks: the directory says which question a hook
answers, and only EXECUTABLE files are consulted. See
.BR "THE HOOK SEAMS" .
.TP
.I $VALET_KEY_CONFIG/context
The RETIRED single-file hook, replaced by the two directories above. Nothing
reads it;
.B doctor
fails if one is still present, because a file that looks wired while enforcing
nothing is worse than no file at all.
.TP
.I $VALET_KEY_CONFIG/profiles
The built-in directory resolver: lines
.RI < profile >
.RI < dir >;
when the context (git root, else
.BR $PWD )
sits under
.IR dir ,
that profile is chosen (first match wins). Consulted only when every
.I profile.d
hook abstains.
.TP
.I $VALET_KEY_CONFIG/dirs
Per-profile config-dir overrides: lines
.RI < agent >
.RI < profile >
.RI < dir >,
pinning that pool's directory instead of the
.RI < base >- profile
default.
.TP
.I $VALET_KEY_POOL_ROOT/<agent>/<profile>/.size
The slot count
.B provision
was last asked for.
.B check
compares the pool against it; a pool without one predates the record, so there
is no declared value to audit and none is invented.
.TP
.I $VALET_KEY_POOL_ROOT/<agent>/<profile>/.reconciled/
When each slot was last brought up to date, so a launch can skip the work when
nothing has changed. valet-key's own bookkeeping, kept OUT of the slots: an
agent's config directory belongs to the agent.
.TP
.I $VALET_KEY_POOL_ROOT/<agent>/<profile>/slot-N
The slot directories. A slot is
.I slot-
followed by digits and nothing else. An agent may create its own lock
directory beside the slot it is using; such a sibling is not a slot, and is
not counted as one.
.IP
Each slot is a thin view of that profile's base config: subdirectories and the
adapter's
.B ADAPTER_STATIC_FILES
are SYMLINKS back to the base, so work done in a slot lands in the real
account; the
.B ADAPTER_SEED_FILES
are private copies carrying no token; the
.B ADAPTER_CRED_FILE
is private and never seeded, which is the whole point; and lock files are
private too \(em a shared lock would let one slot's stalled token refresh
wedge every session at once.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set up standalone with two profiles:
.PP
.nf
.RS
eval "$(valet-key init)"          # shims dir first on PATH
valet-key shim claude
printf 'work ~/work\en' > ~/.config/valet-key/profiles
valet-key provision claude personal
valet-key provision claude work
.RE
.fi
.PP
Now
.B claude
run under
.I ~/work
uses the
.I work
profile and its own slot pool; anywhere else,
.IR personal .
.PP
Warm a couple of slots so the first sessions do not each prompt a login:
.PP
.nf
.RS
valet-key login          # warm the next cold slot
valet-key login          # and another
valet-key login check    # warm/total and days-to-cap
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR mux (1)
.PP
valet-key is developed alongside, but independent of,
.BR mux :
mux merely launches an agent shim; there is no code dependency.
